# Copied to .gitattributes in the published repository by scripts/publish.sh.
#
# Named without the leading dot on purpose, for the same reason publish/gitignore is: a real .gitattributes in publish/ would apply to this directory of the source repository rather than to the published one.

# Normalise line endings in the repository to LF, whatever the machine committing them uses.
#
# This is here for the students rather than for tidiness. Git for Windows sets core.autocrlf=true in its installer by default, so a student on Windows checks this repository out with CRLF; without this line, whether those endings come back on commit depends on settings none of them have looked at, and the result is a diff claiming every line of a file changed when they edited one.
# Modules 1 and 2 of this course are taught from the Modern DevOps site and are where a student first reads a diff, so a lying diff lands at the exact moment they are being asked to trust one.
#
# `text=auto` lets git decide what is text by inspecting content, so a binary that is not matched below is still safe.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts must be LF even if something above ever changes: CR at the end of a shebang line makes the kernel look for an interpreter whose name ends in a carriage return, and the error message names a file that visibly exists.
*.sh    text eol=lf

# The things a reader actually opens here. Declared rather than left to detection so that a file which happens to begin with an unlucky byte is not guessed wrong.
*.md    text eol=lf
*.qmd   text eol=lf
*.tex   text eol=lf
*.html  text eol=lf
*.css   text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.toml  text eol=lf
*.svg   text eol=lf

# A notebook is json, so it is text and it should diff as text --- which is half of what makes the .ipynb worth shipping at all --- but it must not be touched by anything that rewrites it.
*.ipynb text eol=lf

# Binary. Marked explicitly so git never tries to normalise or merge them, and never prints "binary files differ" as though it had guessed.
*.pdf   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary

# docs/ is generated by scripts/deploy.sh in the source repository and rsynced here. Telling GitHub so keeps the repository's language bar from reading "HTML 99%", which is a fact about Quarto's output rather than about this course, and keeps the built site out of diffs by default in pull requests.
docs/** linguist-generated=true
